# tsk demo

This file is the living showcase of every implemented feature. Open it in the
Extension Development Host (`F5` from this folder) to see grammar highlighting,
decorations, code lenses, and commands in action.

## Cache (M3)

On activation, the extension scans every `**/*.tsk` file in the workspace
(excluding `**/node_modules/**`) and indexes tasks, metadata, and tags into
a SQLite cache at `${workspaceFolder}/.vscode/tsk-cache.db` (configurable
via `tsk.cache.path`; opens in-memory when no workspace folder is open).
The `tsk` Output channel logs a `initial scan: N scanned, … tasks indexed,
… tags, …ms` line at completion.

Two surfaces report problems at scan time, per the **Warnings convention**:

- The Output channel — chronological log, click any `file:line` to navigate.
- The Problems panel — `Warning` diagnostics squiggle the offending line.

Today's warning categories: duplicate `@id` across the workspace, and tasks
with no `@id` at all. Both prevent the offending task from participating in
codelens / tag-find / graph lookups.

Open the Command Palette and run **Tsk: Rebuild Cache** to wipe and rescan
(useful after editing files outside VSCode, or if anything ever drifts).

## Decorations (M4)

Two decoration layers on `.tsk` documents:

- **Marker triplet color** — each `[X]` lights up per-marker via the
  `tsk.marker.{inprogress,completed,moved,cancelled,notes}` theme colors
  contributed in `package.json`. `todo` (`[ ]`) deliberately uses the
  editor's default foreground; the other five get distinct hues. `completed`
  and `cancelled` also strike through their triplet to read as "done /
  abandoned" at a glance. Override colors per workspace via
  `workbench.colorCustomizations` if the defaults clash with your theme.
- **Priority line background** — `@priority:1` paints the whole line red,
  `:2` yellow, `:3` blue. Opacity is settable via
  `tsk.decorations.priority.opacity` (default `0.15`); flipping it triggers
  a live rebuild of the decoration types — no reload needed.
- **Dimmed inline metadata** — every `<!-- @key:value -->` block is colored
  via `tsk.metadata.foreground` (a dim gray with alpha) so the comments
  recede into the editor background. The parsed values surface via
  hover-on-task in Phase 2; for now the dimming is the "present but quiet"
  hint that those comments are extension-managed bookkeeping, not content.

Decorations apply on editor focus, on save, and on a 300 ms debounce after
text changes; the timer is independent of the cache rescan debounce.

### Priority examples

- [ ] urgent — fix the data corruption #project/tsk <!-- @id:d4p1 @priority:1 -->
- [ ] important — investigate the slow query #project/tsk <!-- @id:d4p2 @priority:2 -->
- [ ] background — tidy up the readme #project/tsk <!-- @id:d4p3 @priority:3 -->
- [ ] no priority — a normal task <!-- @id:d4none -->

Try editing the opacity in `settings.json` while these are visible:

```json
{ "tsk.decorations.priority.opacity": 0.4 }
```

The line backgrounds intensify (or fade) immediately.

## Markdown still works

Headings, **bold**, *italic*, `inline code`, and links all render via the embedded
markdown grammar.

```ts
// Fenced code blocks are highlighted by the embedded TypeScript grammar.
function hello(name: string): string {
    return `hi ${name}`;
}
```

```sh
echo "shell blocks too"
```

> Block quotes also work — markdown features are not lost.

## Task markers

Every marker character is scoped distinctly, so themes can color them per-marker.
Decorations on top of these scopes arrive in M4.

- [ ] todo — the default empty marker
- [/] in progress — work has started
- [x] completed — done
- [>] moved — relocated somewhere else
- [!] cancelled — abandoned
- [n] notes — informational sub-item

Case tolerance: `[X]` and `[N]` are also accepted on read.

- [X] also completed
- [N] also a note

Indented tasks (nesting) work too:

- [ ] parent task
    - [/] indented child
        - [x] deeply nested
- [ ] sibling

## Inline metadata

Metadata lives inside an HTML comment after the task content. Each `@key:value`
pair is scoped separately so themes can highlight the key, the separator, and
the value.

- [x] write the grammar <!-- @id:k3w8t9rx @created:2026-05-24T15:00:00+08:00 @completed:2026-05-24T17:30:42+08:00 -->
- [/] try a value-less flag <!-- @id:m2x7v4qy @starred -->

## Tags

Tags use `#word` and support `/` for hierarchy. Markdown headings (which start
with `# ` followed by a space) are *not* matched as tags.

- [ ] ship the M1 milestone #project/tsk #JIRAID-123
- [ ] wire up the homelab #inventory/homelab/nas1

